22:05:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Lived experience matters, and people from Bengali communities—including critics within them—are not obligated to romanticize harmful norms or poor living conditions. Criticism of rigid hierarchy, inadequate sanitation, corruption, forced or pressured marriage, gender inequality, or resistance to reform is not “political correctness” to deny; these are real issues where they occur. But many of the people challenging the word “primitive” also live there or come from those communities. The disagreement is often not over whether problems exist, but over whether those problems justify treating a whole culture as fundamentally inferior or incapable of change. A non-academic, plain-language version is: > “I’ve seen parts of rural Bengali society where public services are poor and traditional social expectations are very restrictive. I think those conditions are behind contemporary standards and should change.” That takes your observations seriously. It just avoids making a permanent, sweeping claim about every Bengali person or every part of Bengali culture.
