22:06:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That pattern may be real, but it does not establish a one-way cause from culture to poverty. Poorer, more isolated villages often have fewer schools, jobs, transport links, health services, and legal support. Those conditions can make families more dependent on kin networks and more likely to retain familiar customs. At the same time, some customs—especially those limiting girls’ education, women’s employment, mobility, or free choice—can also reinforce poverty. The relationship can be mutually reinforcing. So a stronger conclusion is: > In some rural Bengali communities, poverty, weak public investment, and restrictive traditional norms may form a cycle: deprivation makes change harder, while certain norms can limit education, autonomy, and economic opportunity. That is a serious critique and can support specific reforms. It is different from claiming that Bengali culture as a whole is the inherent cause of underdevelopment. To show the latter, you would need to separate culture from factors such as land inequality, colonial and postcolonial policy, political governance, migration, schooling, access to markets, and flood or climate vulnerability—and compare similar communities under different conditions.
